options.explanation:
Let's understand the meanings of the parts of this equation.

$\begin{align}
\boxed{\begin{align}
\scriptsize\text{Price per kilogram}&\quad\qquad\scriptsize\text{Number of kilograms}
\\
\scriptsize\text{of white corn}&\quad\qquad\scriptsize\text{of white corn}
\\
&\searrow\quad\qquad\swarrow
\\
&\large \underbrace{\overbrace{0.30}\cdot\overbrace{x}}
\\
&\qquad\uparrow
\\
&\scriptsize\text{Total price of white corn}
\end{align}}
\\\\
\boxed{\begin{align}
\scriptsize\text{Price per kilogram}&\quad\qquad\scriptsize\text{Number of kilograms}
\\
\scriptsize\text{of yellow corn}&\quad\qquad\scriptsize\text{of yellow corn}
\\
&\searrow\quad\qquad\swarrow
\\
&\large \underbrace{\overbrace{0.15}\cdot\overbrace{y}}
\\
&\qquad\uparrow
\\
&\scriptsize\text{Total price of yellow corn}
\end{align}}
\end{align}$

So $0.30x+0.15y$ is the total cost of both types of corn, and we want that to be equal to $\$12$.
